## 1. Scope

This audit covers persistent background jobs, workflow workers, retries, leasing, scheduling, idempotency, readiness probes, operational visibility, and worker deployment wiring. It inspects `product_platform/worker`, `product_platform/workflows/worker.py`, `api/app.py`, `cli.py`, `docker-compose.demo.yml`, job migrations, and frontend workflow views. Relevant benchmarks are Temporal task queues/workers, AWS AgentCore async runtime patterns, and CrewAI deployment/monitoring workflows. Out of scope: saga-specific compensation semantics, covered in SAG.

## 2. Current Implementation Summary

The platform has job tables in the base schema, API models in `worker/api_models.py`, a job store in `worker/store.py`, scheduling logic in `worker/scheduler.py`, workflow execution in `workflows/worker.py`, and API routes around `api/app.py:9530`. Workflow run creation can enqueue jobs around `api/app.py:1824`. The CLI exposes a worker command in `cli.py`, but the shipped loop runs a no-op worker path. `docker-compose.demo.yml` repeats the demo/no-op worker wiring. The frontend has workflow pages but not a full job operations console.

Current workflow: jobs can be created and listed, schedules can produce jobs, some workflow worker code can claim and process workflow jobs, and tests cover basic state transitions. Production queue routing, worker heartbeats, DLQs, idempotency, and operational dashboards are incomplete.

